Ladybird completes console input the way Gecko’s console does in its
WebConsoleActor and jsPropertyProvider(), with the same case rule
(getMatchedPropsImpl() matches without regard to case only when the
typed name starts in lowercase), the same order (lowercase names first,
then the typed name itself, then the rest), array indices left out,
reserved words offered only where no period precedes the name, and
limits of 1500 matches and 100000 properties examined.

Gecko also reads along the chain without running page script: Its
DevToolsUtils.getProperty() calls only a getter that hasSafeGetter()
accepts (a native getter of a web platform object), and isUnsafeGetter()
stops completion at every other getter. But WebKit evaluates the whole
expression before the period, in JavaScriptRuntimeCompletionProvider,
so a getter in it runs, and it orders properties like __proto__ last.
Blink evaluates that expression too, in the evaluateExpression() of its
javascriptCompletionSource, but with throwOnSideEffect, which gives up
on anything with a side effect, and it matches with regard to case.

Gecko’s client also asks to run a getter defined in script once the user
agrees; Ladybird doesn’t offer that, nor completion inside a paused
frame or after a bracket.
